WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.449
As you enter my house my wife and I

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:07.589
wanted to have some kind of artwork that

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:09.000
was personal to the house and also

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.490
related to the work that we do; and so

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.160
you see two things: one

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.050
of them is the dragon curve, I'm gonna explain

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.760
to you more about in a minute, and

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.710
another one is this work here which is a

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.590
grook by Piet Hein. 'Road to wisdom? Well

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.529
it's plain and simple to express. Err and

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:30.570
err and err again but less and less and

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.480
less.' It's one of my 
favorite- it sort of tells

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.989
the story of my life is making mistakes

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:40.020
and trying to learn from that.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.870
They designed it in the 
shape of a superellipse

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:45.660
which is Piet Hein's great

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.230
great discovery of a- of the ellipse that

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.140
that is is more full and has been used

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.079
in in many architectural products. So

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:57.750
what's the idea of the dragon curve? This

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.539
is something invented by a couple of

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:03.960
physicists, late 60s, and you start with a

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.090
piece of paper and you fold it, fold it

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:09.780
again, and you fold it again. And you do

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.080
this n times, where n is nine in the

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:17.159
case of the dragon curve to our left

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:19.979
but here n is four or something. But wh-

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.200
when you look at it you see that some of

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.240
the folds go in mountain folds

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.799
some are valley folds. If you imagine

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:27.509
walking here you're turning left here,

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:28.619
you turning left here, but then you're

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:32.579
turning right; and another 
right turn and a

00:01:32.579 --> 00:01:34.140
left turn and so on. So- so there's a

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.780
pattern of lefts and rights. The amazing

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.450
thing is that if you continue this like

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.520
nine times and then you open all these

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.890
folds up to ninety degree then you get a

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.110
path starts to fill the space and makes

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:51.799
a- quite a nice fractal pattern. So

00:01:51.799 --> 00:01:55.350
start here with- at this tile for example;

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.799
if you if you zoom in on it you'd

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:00.810
that my wife and I had signed our names

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.260
here, but we can

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.630
talk about that story later. But it

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:06.520
starts out and makes a left turn, and a

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.110
left turn, and a right turn,
and a left turn,

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.570
left and so on; so this is exactly the-

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:12.760
the pattern that you get from folding.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.890
Since I folded nine times there's 512

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:19.450
steps to this - well

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:23.290
511 probably - to this pattern. So it ends-

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.830
it ends over here; and it keeps going but

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.850
in the middle it gets very intricate. And

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:34.740
the great thing is though from a

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:38.470
standpoint of geek art, you know, is that

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:41.320
you only need three 
kinds of tiles; a tile

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.020
that's completely blank, or a tile that

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.260
has one loop in it like this one, or a

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.480
tile that has two loops in it like this

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.720
one. And of course I could take the tile

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.430
with two loops and I could tip it this

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.890
way, then it wouldn't be joining from

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:58.720
here to here but it would be- that

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.850
would make this into an isolated circle.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:02.320
All right, yeah, I had to count how many

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.630
blanks- how many
blanks I needed, how many

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.750
single loops and so on. And so we

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.090
made a mock-up in in brown paper and

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.890
cardboard and had it in the apartment

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.300
where we were living while our house was

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.459
being designed. And 
we said, hey, this is a

00:03:15.459 --> 00:03:17.650
cool design, it'd be 
kind of nice to have this

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.870
in our house when when the
house was finally

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:25.150
built. So then we we both loved the tiles

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:27.040
that are made by Heath ceramics in

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.840
Sausalito, California; and we had a

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.790
mock-up of the design so we went up to

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.820
see the Heath ceramics and talked to the

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.400
people there; and we wondered if

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.180
they could make custom tiles for us,

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.410
certain number blank,
certain number of single.

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:46.690
And the answer was, well, we could do it

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:49.269
except that we all- we only make tiles

00:03:49.269 --> 00:03:51.360
that are three and a half inches square.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.080
And what- my design called for two inches

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.950
square. If this- the mathematics could

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.900
not be changed even if I went down to

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.580
8 instead of 9 it wouldn't have

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:08.890
been- it wouldn't have been as interesting

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.440
a pattern, but also it would be huge and

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.720
wouldn't look right at scale. This-

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.640
this kind of pattern is very appealing

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:19.750
at this size but not at a bigger size. So

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:23.890
we were down, we looked very disheartened

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.590
evidently because the person we were

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:29.020
talking to saw that we were very

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.510
disappointed, that 
we had, you know, driven

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:35.530
up here and had this nice idea. But they
said, wait!

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:41.410
One week in June we're going on 
vacation, we

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:43.900
close our factory, and you- but but

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.330
we have workers,
somebody here is a security

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:48.160
person, you could come in and use our

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.100
equipment. We let you use our clay and

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.650
extrude tiles and if you cut them to the

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.210
size, two inches by two inches, then

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:03.910
we'll also let you put on our glazes and

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:06.010
we'll fire the tiles for you afterwards.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:09.580
And so that's- that's what we did, we we

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.630
went up there and spent a day making all

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.270
these tiles, and a few spares, and put our

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.700
initials in this one for the starting

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:21.780
point. Then they fired it and- there are

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.640
some of the accidents in the fire and

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.200
you can see, sometimes it cracked a

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.240
little bit during the during the firing.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.300
So we had- we had nine of these actually

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.950
so I could- so I put those in so that

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:38.530
after- after 8 steps

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:40.990
it's here. And then after 16 you're here,

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:47.370
32 I'm here. So years went by and and I

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:50.680
finally put a chapter in my book on fun

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:54.390
and games that talks about this. And I

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.750
reread all the papers I had written about

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:02.169
the dragon curve and come to a shocking

00:06:02.169 --> 00:06:05.020
discovery; that is that this is wrong. You

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.840
get this by making nine folds but the

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.790
ninth fold I made in the wrong direction,

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:11.830
instead of going this way I went that
way.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:17.290
And so actually this is- the last half of
this

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.780
has a different bend,
and there's something-

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.979
mathematically I've been living with

00:06:23.979 --> 00:06:26.650
this for thirty years and didn't realize

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:28.389
that I had a mathematical error in the

00:06:28.389 --> 00:06:30.470
design.
- (Brady: How do you feel about that now?)

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.770
Well I feel the

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:36.419
moral is: err and err and err again
but less and less and less.

